diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index d532f62..7d04b6c 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -3,17 +3,32 @@
 A small command-line task tracker that stores tasks in a JSON file.
 
 ```
-tasker add [-p N] <title words...>     add a task (priority 1-5, default 3)
-tasker list [--all] [--sort id|priority] list open tasks (or all)
+tasker add [-p N] [--tag T]... [--due YYYY-MM-DD] <title words...>
+                                                  add a task (priority 1-5, default 3)
+tasker list [--all] [--tag T]... [--overdue] [--sort id|priority|due]
+                                                  list open tasks (or all)
 tasker done <id>                        mark a task done
 tasker rm <id>                          delete a task
-tasker edit <id> [-p N] [--title T]     change priority or title
+tasker edit <id> [-p N] [--title T] [--tag T] [--untag T] [--due YYYY-MM-DD|none]
+                                                  change fields
+tasker tags                             list tags used by open tasks with counts
 ```
 
+- `--tag` (repeatable on `add` and `list`, `--tag`/`--untag` on `edit`) attaches
+  tags. Tags are normalised to lower-case and must match `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$`.
+  `list --tag` shows only tasks having ALL given tags.
+- `--due` sets a due date (stored as UTC midnight); `--due none` clears it.
+  `list --overdue` shows open tasks due strictly before today.
+- `list --sort due` orders by due date ascending; tasks without a due date
+  come last, ties by ID.
+- List output appends ` (due YYYY-MM-DD)` and ` [tag1,tag2]` when present,
+  e.g. `#4 [ ] P2 file taxes (due 2026-04-15) [home,money]`.
+
 The data file is `$TASKER_FILE` (default `tasks.json` in the current directory).
+Version-1 files load fine and are upgraded to schema version 2 on save.
 
 ## Layout
-- `internal/model` — the Task type and validation.
+- `internal/model` — the Task type and validation (tags, due dates).
 - `internal/store` — loading/saving the JSON file (with a schema `version`).
 - `internal/query` — filtering and sorting.
 - `internal/cli` — argument parsing, commands and output formatting. `cli.Run` is the testable entry point.
